  "description": "The palm and the palmar fingers. The palmar skin is thick and bound to the palmar aponeurosis by fibrous septa. Under the aponeurosis the superficial palmar arch crosses the palm at about the level of the extended thumb's web, with the common palmar digital arteries and nerves running distally between the flexor tendons to the webs, where they divide into the proper palmar digital vessels and nerves that run along the sides of each finger. Deeper, on the bases of the metacarpals and the interossei, lies the deep palmar arch. The median nerve enters the palm through the carpal tunnel under the flexor retinaculum and gives the recurrent branch to the thenar muscles; the ulnar nerve and artery pass radial to the pisiform through Guyon's canal. The thenar and hypothenar eminences cover the first and fifth metacarpals. Over the fingers the flexor tendons lie in their fibrous sheaths on the phalanges and palmar plates; the pulp of the fingertip covers the tuft of the distal phalanx.",
